## 9. Proposed Step-by-Step Testing Plan

> [!IMPORTANT]
> Every step tests ONE thing. Do not combine steps. Power off between wiring changes.

### Step 1 — ✅ WiFi + LED indicator (ALREADY DONE)
- Current `main.py` does this
- Verify: D2 solid = connected, slow blink = failed, fast = AP mode

### Step 2 — Backend ping from ESP32
- ESP32 makes one HTTP GET to `http://<laptop-ip>:8000/health`
- Print response to Thonny serial console
- Verifies: WiFi → network → FastAPI pipeline works end-to-end

### Step 3 — LED test (4 room LEDs)
- Wire **one LED** first with 220Ω resistor
- Small test script: blink that one LED 3 times
- Then add remaining 3 one by one

### Step 4 — Buzzer test
- Wire buzzer (decide pin — GPIO 23 is cleaner)
- Short beep test script

### Step 5 — HC-SR04 distance test (NO servo yet)
- Wire HC-SR04 with **voltage divider on ECHO**
- Static distance reading — print to console
- Verify numbers are reasonable (not -1 or 0 constantly)

### Step 6 — SG90 servo test (NO ultrasonic)
- Wire servo to GPIO 25 (signal) + 5V VCC with 100µF cap
- Sweep 0° → 180° → 0°, slowly
- Verify no ESP32 reset during movement

### Step 7 — Radar (servo + HC-SR04 combined)
- Combine Step 5 + 6
- Print `{angle, distance}` JSON to console

### Step 8 — ESP32 pushes data to backend
- Add HTTP POST to `/device-data` from radar loop
- Verify FastAPI console receives the data

### Step 9 — Backend commands to ESP32
- FastAPI `/command/pending` endpoint
- ESP32 polls it, executes LED/buzzer commands

### Step 10 — Phone dashboard + WebSocket
- Frontend HTML served by FastAPI
- WebSocket radar rendering
- LED toggle buttons

### Step 11 — Optional sensors (Flame + IR)
- Add KY-026 flame sensor on GPIO 34
- Add FC-51 IR sensor on GPIO 35
- Integrate into JSON payload

### Step 12 — Final integration + demo polish

